            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Momof4OK"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It was delicious. I added jalapeño cream cheese, lime, parsley and green pepper hot sauce then baked it in a cast iron pan on 400° . It came out really flavorful and we loved it. Also, I used ritz crackers instead of flour.",
            "datePublished": "2018-04-28T23:16:01.763Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "The best. I added 1/2 cup of crab meat and 1/4 cup corn meal as suggested by a couple other reviewers. I substituted 1 1/2 cups of frozen corn because I think it's better than canned corn, used self-rising flour rather than all-purpose flour and baking powder, buttermilk rather than regular milk, butter rather than shortening, and topped with maple syrup. Ummmy!",
            "datePublished": "2018-03-21T13:42:38.583Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "barb"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I needed to make this gluten free so used a blend of gluten free flours and added 1/2 teaspoon xanthan gum. The texture was great!\nOnly other changes - I used 2 tablespoons sugar instead of what was in the recipe and only about 8 oz. corn. Was a real treat!",
            "datePublished": "2017-08-07T23:24:54.740Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Delbria"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Spot on! Took me to my childhood and spending summers in Sumter SC with my grandparents. I used 2 cobs of corn, butter, added 2 green onions, TWO tablespoons of sugar and a couple light shakes of nutmeg. \n\nThey came out light and fluffy, soft on the inside with burst of sweet crisp corn. In my opinion green onion and corn pair so well together. Topped with syrup. My daughter used honey. Sooo good! \n\nI obviously deviated from the recipe slightly with my additions. However, the recipe would still be delicious as written",
            "datePublished": "2021-11-11T03:48:41.297Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CopperRibs9290"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I am 67 years old and we have been eating these for as long as I can remember. We always deep fry them sprinkle with powdered sugar and then we would put syrup on them or you can dip them in syrup which makes a perfect combination. This is the way my family ate them for years. I hope this helped and don't give up on them a little powdered sugar and syrup will make a big difference.",
            "datePublished": "2024-08-08T04:02:26.137Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CleverFlan5193"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I recently tried making this and decided to adjust the recipe to better suit my taste preferences. Instead of using granulated sugar, I swapped in powdered sugar for a smoother sweetness. To enhance the flavor, I added a full teaspoon of salt, along with similar amounts of onion powder and garlic powder. The results? Absolutely delicious! The powdered sugar gave the fritters a subtle sweetness that balanced perfectly with the savory notes. The salt, onion powder, and garlic powder combined beautifully, adding depth and a savory kick to each bite. The seasoning was just right without overpowering the natural flavor of the corn. The fritters turned out crispy on the outside, tender on the inside, and bursting with flavor. This recipe adjustment definitely elevated a classic dish, and it’s one I’ll be keeping in my recipe rotation. Highly recommend trying these tweaks if you like a sweet-savory balance!",
            "datePublished": "2025-01-10T19:22:44.550Z"
          },
          {
